    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d91">

                <pb facs="0300" n="930" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v2/pg930"/>

                <head>No. 397.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">General Sickles to Mr. Fish.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">United
                                States Legation in Spain</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Madrid</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-03-14"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 14, 1873</date>.
                        (Received April 4.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 554.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p>Sir: On the receipt of your cable instruction of the 6th instant I addressed a
                    note to the minister of state, a copy of which, Appendix A, <pb facs="0301"
                        n="931" xml:id="pg931"/> is inclosed, communicating to the government of the
                    republic the congratulations tendered by Congress to the people of Spain.</p>
                <p>On the following day I received from Mr. Castelar the reply,
                    translated in Appendix B.</p>
                <p>The minister has since intimated to me that the government intends proposing to
                    the national assembly a suitable answer, to be made in the name of that body. As
                    the relations between the executive and the present assembly do not seem as
                    cordial as might be desired, it is not improbable the government may reserve the
                    matter for the action of the Cortes Constituyentes.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>D. E. SICKLES.</signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d91-a1.xml" xpointer="d91-a1"/>
